{"causes":["The 1960s space program by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)","The need for reliable food safety methods during space missions","The increasing awareness of foodborne illnesses"],"effects":["Reduced risk of foodborne illnesses worldwide","Improved food safety practices in the industry","Compliance with food safety regulations"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration","sourceName":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"NASA developed the HACCP approach as a method for ensuring food safety during space missions."},{"sourceSlug":"United States","sourceName":"United States","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"The US government has recognized the importance of HACCP in reducing foodborne illnesses."},{"sourceSlug":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","sourceName":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"FAO has endorsed HACCP as a global standard for ensuring food safety."},{"sourceSlug":"European Union","sourceName":"European Union","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"The EU has implemented HACCP regulations to ensure food safety in the member states."},{"sourceSlug":"Food Safety Modernization Act","sourceName":"Food Safety Modernization Act","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"The US FDA requires HACCP implementation for food manufacturers."},{"sourceSlug":"International Organization for Standardization","sourceName":"International Organization for Standardization","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"ISO has published HACCP standards for food safety management."},{"sourceSlug":"World Health Organization","sourceName":"World Health Organization","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"WHO has recognized HACCP as a key factor in reducing foodborne illnesses."},{"sourceSlug":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","sourceName":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"FAO has endorsed HACCP as a global standard for ensuring food safety."},{"sourceSlug":"United Nations","sourceName":"United Nations","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"The UN has recognized the importance of HACCP in reducing foodborne illnesses."}],"places":[{"name":"United States","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"European Union","role":"Regulation"},{"name":"United Nations","role":"Recognition"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: hazard analysis and critical control points","type":"Reference article","slug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q279454","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazard_Analysis_Critical_Control_Point"],"tags":["methodology","Political & Polemical Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"hazard analysis and critical control points contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points (HACCP) is a systematic preventive food safety management system that identifies, evaluates, and controls hazards (biological, chemical, physical) significant to food safety throughout the food production process — from raw material production through processing, distribution, and consumption. HACCP was developed jointly by the Pillsbury Company, the US Army Natick Laboratories, and NASA in the late 1950s–1960s as part of NASA's requirement for zero-defect food for the manned space programme — astronauts could not risk food poisoning in space — and was first presented publicly by Howard Bauman (Pillsbury's food safety director) at the 1971 National Conference on Food Protection. The system is based on seven principles: identify hazards, determine critical control points (CCPs), establish critical limits, establish monitoring procedures, establish corrective actions, establish verification procedures, and establish documentation.

HACCP represented a revolutionary shift in food safety philosophy — from end-product testing (testing finished food for contamination, which is reactive, statistically limited, and economically wasteful) to process control (identifying the specific points in the production process where hazards can be controlled, monitoring those points, and taking corrective action when critical limits are exceeded). This preventive, process-based approach — with its emphasis on systematic hazard analysis and documented control — proved far more effective at ensuring food safety than the traditional inspection-and-testing model.

HACCP has become the international standard for food safety management — incorporated into the Codex Alimentarius (the UN/WHO international food standards body) in 1993, mandated by the EU for all food businesses from 1995 (Directive 93/43/EEC) and by the US FDA for seafood (1997), meat and poultry, and most recently through the Food Safety Modernization Act (FSMA, 2011). Its systematic, documented approach has influenced the development of ISO 22000 (Food Safety Management Systems) and of analogous hazard analysis approaches in pharmaceutical manufacturing, medical device production, and other industries where process safety is critical.","subjects":["Food Safety","Modern Era","Systems Engineering","NASA","Public Health","Food Industry","Regulatory Standards","Operations Research"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"HACCP has become a widely adopted global standard for ensuring food safety, reducing foodborne illnesses, and improving public health.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
